<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghadir Khumm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/632">632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghadir Khumm was an event of 16 March 632, shortly before Muhammad's death, at a pond near Juhfa between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, on the return from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farewell-pilgrimage">Farewell Pilgrimage</a>. Muhammad took Ali by the hand and said, &quot;For whoever I am his mawla, Ali is his mawla.&quot; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia Muslims both accept that this was said, but differ on its meaning. Shia Muslims understand mawla as authority and take it as Muhammad's designation of Ali as successor and first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a>, commemorated on 18 Dhu al-Hijjah as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eid-al-ghadir">Eid al-Ghadir</a>. Sunni Muslims read it as an expression of friendship and esteem rather than a designation of a successor.</p></div>
